 <h1>Exercise Name: High-Foot Placement Leg Press (Glute & Hamstring Focused)</h1>
 <u><b><h4>Exercise Overview</h4></b></u>
This 3D anatomical visualization demonstrates a High-Foot Placement Leg Press. The video highlights the back of the legs—specifically the glutes and hamstrings—in bright color to show how placing your feet higher up on the footplate shifts emphasis away from the quadriceps and onto the posterior chain.

Exercise Name: High-Foot Placement Leg Press (Glute & Hamstring Focused)

Target Muscles: Glutes (Gluteus Maximus) and Hamstrings (Biceps Femoris, Semitendinosus, Semimembranosus)

Secondary Muscles: Quadriceps, Adductors
</div></div>
<pre>   
<h1>How to Perform (Step-by-Step)</h1><u><b><h4>1. Setup</h4></b></u>
Sit on the leg press machine with your upper back and head firmly pressed against the backrest. Place your feet high on the footplate, roughly shoulder-width apart.
 <u><b><h4>2. The Descent (Eccentric Phase)</h4></b></u>
Disengage the safety latches. Inhale and slowly lower the sled toward your torso by bending your knees until your legs form a 90-degree angle. Keep your lower back and glutes flat against the pad throughout the movement.
 <u><b><h4>3. The Press (Concentric Phase)</h4></b></u>
Exhale and drive through your heels to press the platform upward. Stop just before full leg extension to keep continuous tension on the glutes and protect your knees.



<h1>Key Benefits</h1>

 <b>◉Posterior Chain Activation:</b> Placing feet higher on the board increases hip flexion and decreases knee flexion, driving more engagement into the glutes and hamstrings.
 <b>◉Knee-Friendly:</b> Reduces shear force on the kneecaps compared to standard low-foot leg press variations.
 <b>◉Heavy Posterior Loading:</b> Allows you to overload your glutes and hamstrings safely without putting stress on your spine.


<h1>Recommended Sets & Reps</h1>

| Goal | Sets | Reps | Rest |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Muscle Building (Hypertrophy) | 3 – 4 | 8 – 12 | 90 seconds |
| Endurance / Tone | 3 – 4 | 12 – 15 | 60 seconds |
| Strength | 4 – 5 | 6 – 8 | 2 – 3 minutes |

Drive directly through your heels rather than your toes to maximize hamstring and glute activation on every rep.
